O("heart","Heart","organ",0.12,"The heart is a four-chambered muscular pump that moves about 5 liters of blood per minute.","Your heart is a fist-sized muscle that squeezes about 100,000 times a day to push blood around your body.","It has two upper chambers (atria) and two lower chambers (ventricles). The right side sends blood to the lungs; the left side sends it to the rest of the body. Valves keep blood flowing one way.","A pacemaker region, the sinoatrial node, starts each beat. The impulse spreads across the atria, pauses at the AV node, then travels via the His-Purkinje system so the ventricles contract from the apex upward. Cardiac muscle is fed by the coronary arteries during diastole.",[("Mass","≈ 250-350 g"),("Rest heart rate","≈ 60-100 bpm"),("Stroke volume","≈ 70 mL"),("Beats in a lifetime","≈ 2.5-3×10^9")],"The heart starts beating about 3 weeks after conception, before most other organs exist.",[CV],["cardiac_muscle_tissue","dense_connective_tissue"],["cardiac_cycle","blood_clotting"],mesh="heart",kn=("blood_circulation",),oq=["Can the adult human heart be induced to regenerate substantial muscle after injury?"])
