for i,t,sc,sm,b,d,e,at,lab in [
("right_atrium","Right atrium",0.05,"Upper right chamber that receives oxygen-poor blood from the body.","It collects blood returning from the body via the vena cava.","Contains the SA node near the superior vena cava opening and pumps blood to the right ventricle.","Thin-walled; pectinate muscles; fossa ovalis is the remnant of the fetal foramen ovale; receives the coronary sinus.",(-0.03,0.025,0.01),"Right atrium"),
("left_atrium","Left atrium",0.05,"Upper left chamber that receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs.","Freshly oxygenated blood from the lungs arrives here.","Four pulmonary veins drain into it; the left atrial appendage is a small pouch.","Located posteriorly; atrial fibrillation often arises from pulmonary vein ectopy and leads to clots in the appendage.",(0.02,0.03,-0.03),"Left atrium"),
("right_ventricle","Right ventricle",0.06,"Lower right chamber that pumps blood to the lungs.","This chamber pushes blood into the lungs, a short low-pressure trip.","Its wall is thin relative to the left because pulmonary pressure is low.","Crescentic shape wrapped around the septum; peak systolic pressure ≈ 25 mmHg; moderator band carries conduction fibers.",(-0.01,-0.02,0.03),"Right ventricle"),
("left_ventricle","Left ventricle",0.07,"The strongest chamber: it pumps blood to the whole body.","The thick-walled chamber that sends blood out to the body at high pressure.","Its muscle wall is about 1 cm thick and generates around 120 mmHg in systole.","Conical with helical fiber orientation producing twist; ejection fraction normally 55-70%; supplies coronary perfusion in diastole.",(0.03,-0.015,-0.005),"Left ventricle"),
("interventricular_septum","Interventricular septum",0.08,"The muscular wall separating the left and right ventricles.","This wall keeps oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood apart.","It is mostly thick muscle with a small membranous part at the top.","Carries the bundle branches; ventricular septal defects are among the commonest congenital heart anomalies.",(0.005,-0.01,0.01),"Septum"),
("tricuspid_valve","Tricuspid valve",0.03,"Valve between right atrium and right ventricle.","A one-way door with three flaps.","Tied to the ventricle wall by thin cords so it does not flip backward.","Chordae tendineae and papillary muscles prevent prolapse; annulus ≈ 10-11 cm circumference.",(-0.015,0.0,0.015),"Tricuspid valve"),
("mitral_valve","Mitral valve",0.03,"Valve between left atrium and left ventricle.","A two-flap door named for its resemblance to a bishop's hat.","It closes when the ventricle squeezes, producing part of the 'lub' sound.","Bicuspid with anterior and posterior leaflets; mitral regurgitation and stenosis are common valve diseases.",(0.02,0.005,-0.01),"Mitral valve"),
("aortic_valve","Aortic valve",0.025,"Valve between left ventricle and aorta.","This three-cup valve opens as blood is pushed out to the body.","Its cups fill and close the valve when the ventricle relaxes, making the 'dub' sound.","Sinuses of Valsalva give rise to the coronary arteries; aortic stenosis is common with aging.",(0.005,0.015,0.005),"Aortic valve"),
("pulmonary_valve","Pulmonary valve",0.025,"Valve between right ventricle and pulmonary artery.","Another three-cup valve letting blood leave toward the lungs.","Prevents backflow into the right ventricle.","A semilunar valve at the top of the right ventricular outflow tract, with three cusps that close under back-pressure.",(0.0,0.025,0.03),"Pulmonary valve"),
("sa_node","Sinoatrial (SA) node",0.015,"The heart's natural pacemaker.","A cluster of special cells in the right atrium that sets the heartbeat rhythm.","Its cells spontaneously fire about 60-100 times per minute.","Pacemaker cells use the funny current (If, HCN4) and calcium clock; autonomic input modulates rate.",(-0.035,0.045,0.005),"SA node"),
("av_node","Atrioventricular (AV) node",0.01,"A relay that delays the heartbeat signal before it reaches the ventricles.","The signal pauses here for a fraction of a second so the atria can finish emptying.","It is the only normal electrical connection between atria and ventricles.","Slow conduction (≈ 0.05 m/s) from calcium-channel-based action potentials; secondary pacemaker (40-60 bpm).",(-0.005,0.0,-0.012),"AV node"),
("coronary_arteries","Coronary arteries",0.004,"The arteries that feed the heart muscle itself.","The heart needs its own blood supply, delivered by arteries on its surface.","Left and right coronary arteries start just above the aortic valve and branch around the heart.","Mostly filled in diastole; blockage by atherosclerosis causes angina and myocardial infarction. Coronary flow ≈ 250 mL/min at rest.",(0.0,0.0,0.045),"Coronary arteries"),
]:
    O(i,t,"structure",sc,sm,b,d,e,[("Role",sm.rstrip('.'))],"The heart beats about 100,000 times each day." if False else {"right_atrium":"The right atrium contains the heart's natural pacemaker.","left_atrium":"The left atrium's appendage is where most atrial-fibrillation clots form.","right_ventricle":"Its output equals the left ventricle's beat for beat, though at lower pressure.","left_ventricle":"The left ventricle alone makes the pulse you feel.","interventricular_septum":"Both ventricles share this wall and push against each other slightly with each beat.","tricuspid_valve":"Its name just means 'three points'.","mitral_valve":"Named after a bishop's mitre.","aortic_valve":"It opens and closes about 100,000 times a day.","pulmonary_valve":"In the Ross procedure, a surgeon swaps a diseased aortic valve with the patient's own pulmonary valve.","sa_node":"Without an SA node signal, other cells take over at a slower pace.","av_node":"The AV node holds the signal for about a tenth of a second.","coronary_arteries":"The heart muscle receives most of its blood only while it is relaxed."}[i],[H],["cardiac_muscle_tissue"] if i not in("tricuspid_valve","mitral_valve","aortic_valve","pulmonary_valve","coronary_arteries") else (["dense_connective_tissue"] if "valve" in i else ["smooth_muscle_tissue","epithelial_tissue"]),["cardiac_cycle"],hs=(H,at,lab))
